WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:10.670
Humans are now living longer than ever before.

00:00:10.670 --> 00:00:16.700
In just a century American life expectancy
has gone from forty seven to seventy nine

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:17.700
years.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:18.860
In one century.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:24.720
Today scientists are growing hearts in the
lab, creating organs with 3-D bioprinters,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.609
body organs and eliminating cells that shorten
life.

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:30.310
Eliminating them.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:35.160
Will this new technology yield another dramatic
increase in life expectancy?

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:36.160
Is it a good idea?

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:38.450
That's a question that some people are asking.

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.080
So let me introduce our guests.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:46.470
Our first guest is the director of regenerative
medical research and director of the Center

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:51.299
for cell and organ biotechnology at the Texas
Heart Institute in Houston.

00:00:51.299 --> 00:00:59.510
Please welcome Doris Taylor.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:05.100
Our next guest is the director of the Robert
and Arlene Kogod Center on Aging at the Mayo

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.430
Clinic and Noaber Foundation Professor of
Aging Research.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:17.260
Please welcome James L. Kirkland.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.460
Our next guest is a novelist, a blogger, a
syndicated columnist and media commentator

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:26.370
and expert in international affairs and biotechnology
policy.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:35.140
Ladies and gentlemen: Jamie Metzl.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.530
And our final guest for this evening is the
William E. William Davis Jr. M.D. Professor

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:44.920
of Medical Ethics and chief of the division
of medical ethics at Weill Cornell Medical

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:52.880
College, Please welcome Joseph Fins.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:54.110
And we're going to start with the question.

00:01:54.110 --> 00:01:58.180
What is biological aging and why should we
study it.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.710
Let me start with you Doris.

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:06.330
Well I think biologic aging is really a function
of the age of our cells.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:15.160
You know I may, I may act twenty some day
but my cells know they're not.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:23.850
That being said I think biologic aging is
really a culmination of stress, emotional,

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:32.600
mental, physical parameters that impact every
aspect of our body and including not just

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:36.910
ourselves, but our organs and tissues.

00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:37.990
James

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:45.540
I think one of the views in the field is that
there are a number of processes which determine

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.530
your physiologic age or your biologic age
as opposed to your chronological age which

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.490
can be quite the distinct.

00:02:52.490 --> 00:03:00.860
So when you think of children they're really
quite similar at a particular chronological

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:05.290
age you know a kid in kindergarten is very
much like another kid in kindergarten and

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:10.069
they view a kid in grade one as being a lot
older than they are almost the same age as

00:03:10.069 --> 00:03:11.069
their parents.

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:14.620
When people are in their twenties they're
becoming a little more distinct but they maybe

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:21.020
at the same level of university and but there
are some differences appearing.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.270
When you get to people in their eighties and
nineties because I'm, among other things,

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:29.090
I'm a geriatrician, I see these people.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:33.709
You know many of us are approaching that age
in our parents and grandparents have been

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:36.900
through this, there are some eighty five year
olds who are out there playing thirty-six

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.950
holes of golf a day and others who have twenty
different conditions and are in thirty different

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:42.950
drugs.

00:03:42.950 --> 00:03:49.489
So biologic age and chronological age diverge
quite a bit as you get older and really what

00:03:49.489 --> 00:03:54.420
biological age predisposes to are the major
age related chronic diseases.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:04:00.709
The highest risk factor for having a heart
attack for example is your age and it's more

00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:02.450
specifically your biological age.

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:07.400
And a brief one sentence definition then of
biological age?

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:14.069
Well at the fundamental level there are, you
can bin or group these processes in various

00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:15.069
different ways.

00:04:15.069 --> 00:04:20.889
But one way I simplistically like to think
of it that applies across many organisms is

00:04:20.889 --> 00:04:27.020
a degree of inflammation of tissues, cellular
senescence, which we'll probably talk again

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:28.699
about later.

00:04:28.699 --> 00:04:33.360
Problems with large molecules like DNA, proteins,
lipids, sugars and also organelles within

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:36.949
cells and then stem and progenitor cell disfunction.

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:40.409
You find these processes are highly linked
to each other.

00:04:40.409 --> 00:04:45.909
You use drugs that target one, you tend to
affect the others or single gene mutation.

00:04:45.909 --> 00:04:50.189
And they tend to be conserved across many
species although not every process is conserved

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:51.419
in all species.

00:04:51.419 --> 00:04:52.419
All right.

00:04:52.419 --> 00:04:54.050
We'll come back to that.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:55.139
Jamie?

00:04:55.139 --> 00:04:59.550
Because aging is very complex and multi-factorial,
it's really hard.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:02.879
There's no standard definition of what biological
age is.

00:05:02.879 --> 00:05:06.819
We know, as Jim said, that different people
age at different rate.

00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:11.099
We know that different parts of our body age
at different rates.

00:05:11.099 --> 00:05:16.419
And so while there are these two things, chronological
age and biologically, one of the big challenges

00:05:16.419 --> 00:05:21.960
in this field is to establish some kind of
uniformity for what biological age is.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.249
And the reason that's important is because
we're going to talk about these different

00:05:25.249 --> 00:05:30.919
types of interventions, but we need to be
able to benchmark what is succeeding and what

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:31.919
is not succeeding.

00:05:31.919 --> 00:05:38.120
And so biological age, we can understand it
conceptually but really making it real in

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:42.400
a way it's going to allow us to compare different
types of treatment, that's the gold standard

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:43.589
and we're not there yet.

00:05:43.589 --> 00:05:44.589
Joe?

00:05:44.589 --> 00:05:48.360
I think age is not just a biological construct;
it's a social construct.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:54.669
And when you start expanding the life cycle
and you start interrupting one person's life

00:05:54.669 --> 00:06:00.259
with another life in a sequence of generational
issues I think we're going to have to talk

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:02.729
about what it means for social disruption.

00:06:02.729 --> 00:06:03.729
And…

00:06:03.729 --> 00:06:05.960
What do you mean social disruption?

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:10.900
Well I mean you know, you know as a faculty
member in a medical school you know the tenure

00:06:10.900 --> 00:06:13.259
age keeps on going older and older and older.

00:06:13.259 --> 00:06:16.930
There's a whole cohort of adjuncts who want
my job, right.

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:17.930
So.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:21.899
So that's what happens when you expand the
life expectancy of professors.

00:06:21.899 --> 00:06:23.719
And is that a good thing for the Academy?

00:06:23.719 --> 00:06:25.509
Or is it a good thing for society?

00:06:25.509 --> 00:06:31.090
So I think my sense of it is, I'm a big fan
of the work that folks are doing here, but

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:34.750
I think a useful construct is what we call
fair innings.

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:39.569
You know that if somebody has a life that's
shortened because of a premature illness and

00:06:39.569 --> 00:06:43.431
they're not going to get their full nine innings
you want to, you know, extend the life to

00:06:43.431 --> 00:06:44.431
fair innings.

00:06:44.431 --> 00:06:48.439
But when you start getting into extra inning
games the next day is really difficult when

00:06:48.439 --> 00:06:49.659
you have to play again.

00:06:49.659 --> 00:06:53.879
So I just think you need to think about the
context, the broader context of what when

00:06:53.879 --> 00:06:58.300
we change individuals and we change a lot
of individuals what are we doing to the group?

00:06:58.300 --> 00:06:59.300
What are we doing to society?

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:02.089
I think that's a really important point about
social constructs.

00:07:02.089 --> 00:07:03.089
Absolutely.

00:07:03.089 --> 00:07:06.229
But how do we think about what the social
construct is?

00:07:06.229 --> 00:07:09.219
At the time of the Roman Empire the average
life expectancy was twenty-five.

00:07:09.219 --> 00:07:13.210
As you said Bill in the introduction, a hundred
years ago it was forty-seven.

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:14.210
Right.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:16.449
And everything was benchmarked against that
expectation.

00:07:16.449 --> 00:07:19.210
It's like in Roman times, ‘oh my god you've
made it to 40!’

00:07:19.210 --> 00:07:21.960
That was like a huge accomplishment.

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:27.119
And so I also think we have all of these structures
that are just based on this one historic moment.

00:07:27.119 --> 00:07:29.529
And as things change those structures will
also change.

00:07:29.529 --> 00:07:33.710
I think that just quickly I think there's
a question of radical disruption and how fast

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:34.979
can you accommodate the change.

00:07:34.979 --> 00:07:39.050
And the other question is who will get the
enhancement?

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:44.219
I was just going to say that I think a lot
of what many of us are trying to do is not

00:07:44.219 --> 00:07:48.819
necessarily extend life for those extra innings.

00:07:48.819 --> 00:07:52.529
It's to extend health through those fair innings.

00:07:52.529 --> 00:07:59.279
That really the aspects of longevity that
we want to talk about are living healthy longer,

00:07:59.279 --> 00:08:06.430
as well as you know, the kids who call me
and say, can you keep my mom alive or the

00:08:06.430 --> 00:08:09.210
parents who call me and say, can you help
my son right?

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:14.209
And I totally agree but the title of this
session is "Engineering Immortality" right?

00:08:14.209 --> 00:08:19.649
So you know I think we need to understand
that there's a there's a kind of a medical

00:08:19.649 --> 00:08:26.050
sort of Hippocratic intent and that how it's
understood that we're going to live far longer

00:08:26.050 --> 00:08:27.050
than.

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:28.050
It's true.

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:32.419
But Doris said in the green room, which isn't
actually green, but death sucks.

00:08:32.419 --> 00:08:37.380
And I think that we have been socialized to
accept death and I don't think immortality

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:40.690
is going to be possible for a very long time
if ever.

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:42.810
We've been socialized to accept death.

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:46.200
We've been socialized to define our lives
in relationship to death.

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:50.080
But according again to Doris, death sucks,
and when you think of all the wisdom, all

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:55.140
the love, all the poetry, everything that
goes away when somebody dies if we had the

00:08:55.140 --> 00:09:00.880
opportunity to extend healthy lifespan, and
all of the demographic projections suggest

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:05.450
that around 2050 we're going to start shrinking
again as a global public and we would need

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:07.370
to change social constructs.

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:12.200
But how wonderful if people, people could
meet their great grandparents and maybe we'll

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:17.810
have, you know you lose your tenure at sixty
or seventy or whatever but let's solve for

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:18.840
that problem.

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:23.210
Not say that we have this infrastructure based
on our current life expectancy, therefore

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:28.260
everyone's parents and grandparents have to
die when there's a lot of extra potential.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:32.060
So to get more specific, to move to another
topic.

00:09:32.060 --> 00:09:39.500
Where are we now, you guys who are specialists
in this, as far as targeting the aging process?

00:09:39.500 --> 00:09:44.240
The idea in the field is if we can target
fundamental aging processes, can we delay

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:51.180
the, can we delay prevent or alleviate this
constellation of age related diseases as a

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:54.560
group instead of targeting these things one
at a time.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:59.810
If you cure heart attacks for example you
are choosing to die instead of few months

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:02.490
later of Alzheimer's disease or some cancer.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:04.740
It's a whack a mole situation.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.260
So why not go for these things as a group.

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:12.260
As a clinician what I'm worried about is my
patients having poor quality of life having

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:15.880
onset of these age related disease that cluster
within individuals.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:19.680
If you get one age related disease your time
for the next one is two and a half years.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:21.080
The one after that is a year.

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:22.310
The one after that is six months.

00:10:22.310 --> 00:10:23.430
They cluster in people.

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:24.770
We've all seen that.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:28.270
We need to target that and delay it and that's
what some of these drugs do that seem to work

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:29.530
in mice.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:30.530
Maybe, if we can get them to people.

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:32.490
I want to ask you about that in a minute.

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:33.490
Doris?

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:40.620
So I think that makes sense though endogenous
repair fails and as it fails it doesn't just

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:45.410
fail in your heart, doesn't just fail in your
liver, it fails everywhere.

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:52.650
And part of that is because the capacity for
repair that we have, these cells come down

00:10:52.650 --> 00:11:02.150
as we age and those cells, the more hits you
take, the more you use those cells the fewer

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:08.260
you have the more likely one two three four
five set things are going to fail.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:09.930
But I think that's part of this.

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:14.280
I think the other thing that happens is you
have damaged cells that don't, that aren't

00:11:14.280 --> 00:11:18.510
gotten rid of so there's failure to remove
cells you don't necessarily need to replace

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:21.770
them in some instances, they're damaged cells
that don't get removed.

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:24.430
They can become cancerous.

00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:28.950
They can produce things, senescence cells,
very small numbers of them produce factors

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:30.810
that caused widespread systemic dysfunction.

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:36.770
Do if only one in ten thousand of your cells
is senescent after, when they're transplanted,

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.590
you get, in mice you cause frailty for six
months.

00:11:39.590 --> 00:11:40.590
Causes diabetes,

00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:42.810
What happens when you take out senescence
cells?

00:11:42.810 --> 00:11:44.890
Well you get improvements in some of these
things.

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:47.650
There are there many ways of targeting fundamental
aging processes now.

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:54.440
There at least you know there are well over
a couple of dozen drugs now that target fundamental

00:11:54.440 --> 00:11:59.520
aging processes in mice and there are many
more coming.

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:06.060
The question will be, can we say in an older
person who's got five different medical conditions

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:11.090
can we improve slightly three out of five
of those conditions with a single agent.

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:13.960
Why is slowing or stopping aging a goal?

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:16.560
Why is it a goal?

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.810
Well I can answer that in two words.

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:19.810
Why not?

00:12:19.810 --> 00:12:24.700
I mean if we can live healthy, longer that's
just a pure good.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:30.700
And extending health span and extending lifespan
are correlated, so even if we say our goal

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:37.330
is extending health, and that will very likely,
at least in the early years, extend overall

00:12:37.330 --> 00:12:45.850
lifespan and anybody who's been at the deathbed
of a loved one or a child or a parent and

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:48.840
asks the question ‘why can't we have more
time?’

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:54.400
I think that if we can do that if we can that
was the purpose of health care is alleviating

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:55.400
suffering.

00:12:55.400 --> 00:13:00.800
And one of the failures of all that miraculous
work that's been done in extending lifespan

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:07.690
is that we have more and more people who are
in miserable conditions in geriatric centers

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:08.690
or wherever.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:13.140
But if we can have people live longer, healthier
lives, that’s in my mind just a pure good.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.870
Now you said at the beginning of our conversation,
you said something about we've been conditioned

00:13:16.870 --> 00:13:19.710
to accept death.

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:21.290
Explain that a little bit.

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:25.750
Well you're born and we have all these rituals,
and we when people talk about death I would

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:28.980
imagine most people in this room would say
what's the purpose of death?

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:31.750
Many people would say what gives meaning to
life.

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:35.560
But would you like to have so much meaning
in your life that knowing that you're going

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:40.130
to die two days from now would that really
focus your love of your of your life and the

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:42.350
answer would be no.

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:48.080
So we have all of these rituals and we think
about death and we have all these words that

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:54.230
we say but if any of us had an individual
choice of getting ten healthy years with a

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:57.200
parent, more healthy years of a parent or
a child then everybody would take it.

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.760
Let's just toss an idea here.

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:06.830
I've noticed that many cultures have different
ways of categorizing and surrounding the notion

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:08.920
of death and putting it somewhere safe.

00:14:08.920 --> 00:14:14.970
People who focus on death who are not trained
to do so can get very depressed and it can

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:17.090
increase suicide rates in fact.

00:14:17.090 --> 00:14:24.670
So is there anything, isn't there something
good about having ways to put it off in a

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:25.670
safe place?

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:27.050
Joe you deal with this?

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:32.730
Well this great book by Ernest Becker from
the seventies "The Denial of Death", right?

00:14:32.730 --> 00:14:37.650
And it reminds me of what a European colleague
of mine once said about Americans, he says

00:14:37.650 --> 00:14:39.440
“they don't die they just underachieve.”

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:43.210
And we are a fundamentally death-denying culture.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:47.610
And I think that there's you know I think
no one is against this in a kind of medical

00:14:47.610 --> 00:14:52.610
way to kind of compress morbidity and decrease
the burden of living with chronic disease.

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:58.250
But I think there's a there's a fundamental
illusion of control here that we're not going

00:14:58.250 --> 00:14:59.610
to get rid of death.

00:14:59.610 --> 00:15:03.850
You know it will eventually catch up with
us, death and taxes and I think it is part

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:09.350
of the human fabric and frankly I think that
the fact that we will die gives us some meaning

00:15:09.350 --> 00:15:15.150
to take seriously the time that we have because
it's not an unlimited gift.

00:15:15.150 --> 00:15:19.720
We want it to be a little brighter and shinier
but it's not unlimited And I think we haven't

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:20.720
been sold it.

00:15:20.720 --> 00:15:21.720
It is who we are.

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:23.180
And mortality is part of our lives.

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:24.320
Yeah I agree.

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:30.290
I don't think anybody here is, I don't think
immortality other than downloadable brains,

00:15:30.290 --> 00:15:33.380
which may or may not happen is what anybody
is talking about.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:37.950
But if we can extend healthy lifespan, then
great.

00:15:37.950 --> 00:15:39.960
But let's, let's for a moment.

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:42.220
America especially is noted for this you say.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:44.570
What is it about American life that has made
us deny death?

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:47.240
I think it's extreme individualism.

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:48.240
OK.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:49.310
Almost a kind of Libertarianism.

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:50.310
Control.

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:51.310
Control.

00:15:51.310 --> 00:15:52.310
Control.

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:57.721
And in fact the people who seek assisted suicide
in say Oregon it's not pain, it's wanting

00:15:57.721 --> 00:16:00.160
to be in control of the timing and manner
of their death.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.570
Everyone thought it was for pain and symptom
management.

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:07.070
It's an existential crisis and it's a fundamental
loss of control and I would say that this

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:14.360
is this this effort here is part of that same
social urge to have you know control dominion

00:16:14.360 --> 00:16:18.190
over one's life, one's death and how long
one lives.

00:16:18.190 --> 00:16:23.060
From a psychological point of view, you’ve
got new ways of describing to us when it becomes

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:25.920
unhealthy to deny it.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:32.400
Right, I think, I think what we see this you
know in clinical life where people are asking

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:35.870
for care that at this day and age is impossible.

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:39.529
And you know issues of medical futility.

00:16:39.529 --> 00:16:44.170
Interestingly the word futility comes from
the Latin futilis which means a leaky sieve,

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:48.140
you know it no longer serves its purpose,
it doesn't hold the fluid.

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:53.850
And you know it's amazing how we seek to extend
life.

00:16:53.850 --> 00:16:59.690
And people just can't accept the finality
of it and it leads to tremendous grief.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:06.370
And so you know I think we have to be less
dichotomous about this and appreciate that

00:17:06.370 --> 00:17:10.959
there's a benefit here in compressing morbidity
but we're never going to get rid of our mortality.

00:17:10.959 --> 00:17:16.759
I think we have to talk, to continue to talk
though about quality because, because a number

00:17:16.759 --> 00:17:21.999
of the medical devices that exist at least
in the cardiovascular field, there are a lot

00:17:21.999 --> 00:17:26.780
of people who would say ‘yes I want a device
and I want to live longer.’

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:31.369
And they get that device and they realize
the quality of their life is not as good as

00:17:31.369 --> 00:17:35.889
they thought it was going to be and all of
a sudden the conversation changes.

00:17:35.889 --> 00:17:42.000
And I think we have to be able and willing
to discuss quality.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:45.260
And I mean you see it, this it what you do.

00:17:45.260 --> 00:17:50.500
Don't you deal with people who have quality
issues all the time?

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:58.090
Yeah, as a geriatrician what I see is what
people fear the most is loss of independence

00:17:58.090 --> 00:17:59.110
and control.

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:04.029
And, you know, myself and my colleagues in
geriatrics were sick of providing better wheelchairs,

00:18:04.029 --> 00:18:06.130
walkers and incontinence devices.

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:12.129
So finally the biology of aging in the last,
you know, since around 2009 has moved very

00:18:12.129 --> 00:18:16.490
quickly to developing interventions where
we might be able to enhance healthspan even

00:18:16.490 --> 00:18:20.059
if it's a few months or a couple of years
or something like that.

00:18:20.059 --> 00:18:27.090
It would probably add a lot more in some ways
arguably than curing heart attacks because

00:18:27.090 --> 00:18:31.490
if you cure, if you cure heart attacks you're
choosing instead to die, as I mentioned before

00:18:31.490 --> 00:18:37.049
of Alzheimer’s or some horrible cancer,
because these conditions cluster.

00:18:37.049 --> 00:18:41.369
So our goal is to figure out a way to delay
these things as a group, even if it's a small

00:18:41.369 --> 00:18:44.049
amount, and to try to compress morbidity.

00:18:44.049 --> 00:18:49.190
And these are potentially achievable goals
over the next decade because we can do it

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:52.490
now in mice and other lower mammalian species.

00:18:52.490 --> 00:18:57.639
And the question is can we get this to people
in an incremental way, in a safe way where

00:18:57.639 --> 00:19:01.530
we're dealing with targeting multiple conditions
at once instead of one at a time.

00:19:01.530 --> 00:19:05.149
And that's a really important issue because
right now we have a fundamental problem of

00:19:05.149 --> 00:19:06.409
the misallocation of resources.

00:19:06.409 --> 00:19:08.270
We're doing what Jim called the whack-a-mole.

00:19:08.270 --> 00:19:10.429
Maybe somebody has cancer you go after the
cancer.

00:19:10.429 --> 00:19:13.440
Then they have cardiovascular disease, we
go after that.

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:18.440
But if one thing, if we eliminated all of
these diseases even then people wouldn't live

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:19.440
that much longer.

00:19:19.440 --> 00:19:24.440
But if you get rid of one get rid of all cancer
in the world it's about 3.3 years extended

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:25.940
lifespan.

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:31.369
And so the question is why are we spending
so much on the diseases of aging and so little

00:19:31.369 --> 00:19:34.769
on understanding and targeting aging itself.

00:19:34.769 --> 00:19:35.809
Which is the main risk factor.

00:19:35.809 --> 00:19:36.809
Yeah exactly.

00:19:36.809 --> 00:19:39.369
The upstream cause in a way of these conditions

00:19:39.369 --> 00:19:40.369
Exactly

00:19:40.369 --> 00:19:45.600
So right now, and every one of these diseases
are, they're all terrible and they all have

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:50.899
pressure groups and they all have an infrastructure
for cancer, cardiovascular disease, dementia,

00:19:50.899 --> 00:19:51.899
whatever.

00:19:51.899 --> 00:19:55.990
And what needs to happen, at least in my view,
I think some people up here would share this

00:19:55.990 --> 00:20:00.600
view, is we just need to shift some of that
spending into understanding the basic science

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:01.600
of aging.

00:20:01.600 --> 00:20:06.019
Because from an evolutionary perspective,
we all, everybody has this sense of what is

00:20:06.019 --> 00:20:07.460
and isn't natural.

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:11.149
But the things that we think of as being natural
actually aren't natural at all.

00:20:11.149 --> 00:20:15.909
It's not natural that we live to 80, its just
that our ancestors didn't do it, it's this

00:20:15.909 --> 00:20:18.040
one is the period of time.

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:22.980
There's nothing about evolution that has decided
that we live to 80 because frankly evolution

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:25.559
could care less how long we live.

00:20:25.559 --> 00:20:28.690
If babies are getting eaten by tigers babies
would grow armor.

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:32.379
If parents were getting eaten by tigers and
they couldn't raise their babies, babies would

00:20:32.379 --> 00:20:35.789
become more independent like Komodo dragons
do.

00:20:35.789 --> 00:20:39.159
But if an old person got eaten by a lion,
it was bad for the old person but it didn't

00:20:39.159 --> 00:20:40.929
really touch our evolution as a species.

00:20:40.929 --> 00:20:49.090
So there's a lot of fluidity in how we're
going to be able to age, in my view, how we're

00:20:49.090 --> 00:20:54.559
going to be able to impact and target the
mechanisms of aging and animal models are

00:20:54.559 --> 00:21:00.529
showing as you guys has been mentioned that
there are a lot of things that that can be

00:21:00.529 --> 00:21:01.529
done and I think should be done.

00:21:01.529 --> 00:21:06.059
But I want to speak about animal models, I
feel really strongly about this.

00:21:06.059 --> 00:21:07.960
We can cure anything in mice.

00:21:07.960 --> 00:21:09.399
We can cure cancer in mice.

00:21:09.399 --> 00:21:10.590
We can cure aging in mice.

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:14.419
We can cure heart disease in mice but we can't
cure any of it people.

00:21:14.419 --> 00:21:19.330
And that's because those animal models are
healthy until we give them a heart attack.

00:21:19.330 --> 00:21:22.190
Or they're healthy until we do whatever.

00:21:22.190 --> 00:21:30.480
We don't have models that let us look at old,
sick, taking all these drugs, also have cancer,

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:32.419
also have all these other things.

00:21:32.419 --> 00:21:34.710
Models where we have to test these things.

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:42.110
So I think part of really figuring out how
these work is testing them in real life situations.

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:46.770
And I think it's important to understand women
live longer.

00:21:46.770 --> 00:21:52.650
It varies across species to the gender differences
so that female mice don't outlive male mice.

00:21:52.650 --> 00:21:54.590
They do if they have heart disease.

00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:57.440
It is very situation dependent.

00:21:57.440 --> 00:22:00.269
Generally female mice have the same life span
as male mice.

00:22:00.269 --> 00:22:03.900
In other, some other species males outlive
females for example and some birds.

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:07.649
So this varies and it's peculiarity.

00:22:07.649 --> 00:22:12.049
What, one thing that does seem to be fairly
constant are some of these fundamental aging

00:22:12.049 --> 00:22:17.039
processes from yeast through worms, through
flies, through mice.

00:22:17.039 --> 00:22:20.310
Unlike something like Alzheimer’s disease
which is a uniquely human disease or unlike

00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:25.950
arteriosclerosis, which is almost uniquely
human, does occur in some other species.

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:29.850
Aging, fundamental aging processes tend to
occur across species so there's some hope

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:35.049
and I think we have to have a lot of skepticism
as you're saying until we do clinical trials.

00:22:35.049 --> 00:22:37.700
There's some hope that they might be translatable.

00:22:37.700 --> 00:22:42.759
But as you're saying we have to do the clinical
trials and we have to show this in people

00:22:42.759 --> 00:22:48.519
because you know showing things in mice or
rats or dogs or monkeys doesn't necessarily

00:22:48.519 --> 00:22:49.519
translate.

00:22:49.519 --> 00:22:55.610
Let me see if I can get my aging brain just
to summarize what we've generally been saying

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:56.610
so far.

00:22:56.610 --> 00:23:03.440
You're saying that more than going after individual
diseases, go after aging.

00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:04.610
That's what you all are saying.

00:23:04.610 --> 00:23:05.610
Yes.

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:08.840
What is, what is the future is it regenerative
medicine?

00:23:08.840 --> 00:23:11.830
Or is it a new kind of medicine?

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:16.739
I think, I think it depends on what you're
trying to do.

00:23:16.739 --> 00:23:20.239
If you walk in the door and your legs.

00:23:20.239 --> 00:23:23.690
you've cut off your legs at the knee and you're
bleeding everywhere obviously you have to

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:28.220
deal with that first before you can think
about how old you are and what you got to

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:29.220
do.

00:23:29.220 --> 00:23:34.100
And I think I think some of the disease states
that we've been talking about heart disease

00:23:34.100 --> 00:23:41.049
that you know other chronic diseases we have
to get under control.

00:23:41.049 --> 00:23:46.960
And as we develop new hypotheses about aging
and understand aging.

00:23:46.960 --> 00:23:48.299
I think there's two things.

00:23:48.299 --> 00:23:52.029
I think this falls generally under the rubric
of prevention.

00:23:52.029 --> 00:23:53.029
Right

00:23:53.029 --> 00:23:54.029
Right.

00:23:54.029 --> 00:23:55.179
But it's a kind of secondary prevention.

00:23:55.179 --> 00:23:57.700
After you know that process has already begun.

00:23:57.700 --> 00:24:01.139
People who were you know in their forties
may not have had a heart attack but they may

00:24:01.139 --> 00:24:05.769
have arteriosclerotic changes and could you
reverse that for some you know anti-aging

00:24:05.769 --> 00:24:11.759
intervention versus an earlier public health
intervention that that changes the built environment,

00:24:11.759 --> 00:24:16.899
puts calorie counts on the food, teaches people
about you know high fat diet.

00:24:16.899 --> 00:24:23.149
So I think it's important to realize the built
environment has a lot to do with the process

00:24:23.149 --> 00:24:24.149
of aging.

00:24:24.149 --> 00:24:26.659
Some of it's going to be genetic; some of
it's going to be environmental.

00:24:26.659 --> 00:24:30.691
This is all under the rubric of a kind of
prevention and I would hate for people to

00:24:30.691 --> 00:24:34.490
think they could they could live whatever
life they wanted to live and then when they're

00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:38.659
45 they could go check into the anti-aging
clinic and get some kind of magic bullet.

00:24:38.659 --> 00:24:45.410
When if fact it's better to have a kind of
a multifaceted approach to prevent any morbidity

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:47.539
that's downstream.

00:24:47.539 --> 00:24:51.369
What's the biggest intervention that I can
do right now because I really want to live

00:24:51.369 --> 00:24:52.720
as long as possible?

00:24:52.720 --> 00:24:53.759
It's all these things.

00:24:53.759 --> 00:24:54.859
You need to eat healthy.

00:24:54.859 --> 00:24:57.869
You should exercise 45 minutes a day.

00:24:57.869 --> 00:24:58.869
Don't smoke.

00:24:58.869 --> 00:25:02.000
There's a lot of work on what they call Blue
Zones, which are part of the world where people

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:08.450
live longer and Dan Buettner and others went
and said well what do they all have in common?

00:25:08.450 --> 00:25:14.279
Is it that everybody had that physical activity
built into their lives, they were in communities?

00:25:14.279 --> 00:25:18.889
They had a sense of common purpose and for
whatever reason they ate they all seem to

00:25:18.889 --> 00:25:19.890
eat a lot of beans.

00:25:19.890 --> 00:25:25.330
But having said that everybody should do all
those things.

00:25:25.330 --> 00:25:29.340
But it's also true that in some of the metformin
studies for example they have the two groups

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:30.340
of mice.

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:33.590
One with mice the they ate crap and didn't
exercise.

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:37.249
And one with the mice that they ate good food
and exercised.

00:25:37.249 --> 00:25:43.519
And they gave metformin to the non-exercising,
lazy, fat mice and they outlived the healthy

00:25:43.519 --> 00:25:46.080
mice, so I think that you should do all of
those things.

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:52.200
But then there will also be very likely be
in my view other types of interventions, that

00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:56.500
you'll be better off if you lived a virtuous
life, but will help everybody.

00:25:56.500 --> 00:26:02.200
It'll feel like you're living for seven thousand
years if you’re only living a virtuous life,

00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:03.200
right?

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:05.179
Which some of us enjoy.

00:26:05.179 --> 00:26:12.059
Some of these interventions will see their
first light in early clinical trials in roughly

00:26:12.059 --> 00:26:14.690
a half dozen areas.

00:26:14.690 --> 00:26:17.129
And some of these trials are under way at
the moment.

00:26:17.129 --> 00:26:21.109
So one of the areas is going to be looking
at multi-morbidity.

00:26:21.109 --> 00:26:25.040
So if you've got an eighty five year old who's
got five or six different conditions, can

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:29.289
you partially alleviate three of those conditions
with a single agent?

00:26:29.289 --> 00:26:30.650
So that's one approach.

00:26:30.650 --> 00:26:35.749
The second is going to be in conditions where
they are accelerated aging like states.

00:26:35.749 --> 00:26:39.690
So trials are beginning and people who have
bone marrow transplants and have massive doses

00:26:39.690 --> 00:26:45.230
of chemotherapy and acquired this accelerated
aging like state in three to five years, or

00:26:45.230 --> 00:26:50.149
the kids with progerias who look old when
there when they're young.

00:26:50.149 --> 00:26:55.220
Another area is going to be where there are
localized accumulations of changes that are

00:26:55.220 --> 00:26:58.759
associated with aging like states for example
pulmonary fibrosis, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis

00:26:58.759 --> 00:27:03.669
which is normally fatal without a lung transplant.

00:27:03.669 --> 00:27:07.850
Again we at Mayo recently published a paper
showing that these drugs that clear senescent

00:27:07.850 --> 00:27:14.220
cells radically improve mice in that particular
model and clinical trials are going to begin.

00:27:14.220 --> 00:27:15.220
On humans.

00:27:15.220 --> 00:27:16.220
Yes.

00:27:16.220 --> 00:27:17.220
How soon.

00:27:17.220 --> 00:27:18.220
Very soon.

00:27:18.220 --> 00:27:21.639
Will these kinds of drugs reduce the amount
of chemotherapy that has to be given or reduce

00:27:21.639 --> 00:27:27.859
side effects of chemotherapy or enhanced recovery
after surgery or enhance recovery after pneumonia?

00:27:27.859 --> 00:27:32.210
Will they, and some of them do this now, one
trial's already been done showing that we

00:27:32.210 --> 00:27:38.230
can improve vaccine response in elderly individuals
to flu vaccine by giving these kinds of drugs,

00:27:38.230 --> 00:27:43.750
in this case a drug related to something called
rabomicin, for a few days two weeks before

00:27:43.750 --> 00:27:44.750
they got their flu shot.

00:27:44.750 --> 00:27:48.399
And these were communing, living, healthy
elderly individuals who normally have bad

00:27:48.399 --> 00:27:53.250
response to flu vaccines, and it was greatly
improved by targeting fundamental aging processes.

00:27:53.250 --> 00:27:57.000
So there are a bunch of clinical trial scenarios
that have been gained out over a three-year

00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:03.789
period by a large group of aging centers across
the country and involving Europe that included

00:28:03.789 --> 00:28:06.299
the NIH and the FDA.

00:28:06.299 --> 00:28:11.919
And so they're very short-term trials that
could be done in a short period of time with

00:28:11.919 --> 00:28:16.049
subjects involved in the trials for two to
six months to look at these kinds of interventions

00:28:16.049 --> 00:28:18.350
and see if they work and those trials are
beginning.

00:28:18.350 --> 00:28:23.970
Well this comes to the next question I wanted
to ask about, is have all of you give us a

00:28:23.970 --> 00:28:30.149
sense of what the next twenty or thirty years
in this field look like.

00:28:30.149 --> 00:28:31.149
Where are we going?

00:28:31.149 --> 00:28:36.840
Well, I'm the only science fiction writer
here so I feel the freest to just make stuff

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:39.039
up which I will do.

00:28:39.039 --> 00:28:44.190
So I think in the near term, I'll do it in
three buckets, in the near term all of the

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:49.999
kind of work that we mentioned to this point,
I think some of that will bear fruit.

00:28:49.999 --> 00:28:54.269
Some of it seems very promising now and it
turns out it won't work.

00:28:54.269 --> 00:29:00.179
But there will be a lot of things whether
it's rejuvenating stem cells or pruning senescent

00:29:00.179 --> 00:29:09.659
cells or using blood plasma transfusion to
trigger certain cellular responses or all

00:29:09.659 --> 00:29:10.830
kinds of small molecules.

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:14.640
I mean a lot of these things that are undergoing,
some of them will be realized.

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:16.479
That's the short term

00:29:16.479 --> 00:29:22.639
In the medium to longer term, I think things
are going to get more....

00:29:22.639 --> 00:29:28.870
It's going to be more, maybe ethically challenging
and the kinds of things which people, I think

00:29:28.870 --> 00:29:31.789
everything about these interventions, my guess
is most of you in the audience will say well

00:29:31.789 --> 00:29:35.350
that's pretty good if I’m aging and you
can do this kind of an intervention.

00:29:35.350 --> 00:29:40.299
But in the medium, in the medium term we're
going to use genome sequencing and we're going

00:29:40.299 --> 00:29:46.009
to understand different genetic combinations
that give you a greater shot of living longer.

00:29:46.009 --> 00:29:51.700
I have written a book on the future of human
genetic engineering and embryo selection but

00:29:51.700 --> 00:29:58.149
I think that there will come a time in the
not so distant future when all children, the

00:29:58.149 --> 00:30:03.419
majority of children will be born through
IVF and those embryos will be screened and

00:30:03.419 --> 00:30:06.340
they will be screened for certain outcomes.

00:30:06.340 --> 00:30:12.159
And your possibility of living a long and
healthy and robust life will be one of those

00:30:12.159 --> 00:30:16.649
outcomes that will be screened for at the
time when decisions are being made about which

00:30:16.649 --> 00:30:21.109
embryos, early stage embryos to be implanted
in the mother.

00:30:21.109 --> 00:30:23.410
That would I think that is medium term.

00:30:23.410 --> 00:30:27.309
And longer term, if we're talking hundreds
of years, and again I'm a science fiction

00:30:27.309 --> 00:30:31.700
writer so take this with a grain of salt,
if we do get to the point of downloadable

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:37.600
memory and we can transfer our minds into
some other medium then we'll just have this

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:39.870
fundamental question, ‘is that us’?

00:30:39.870 --> 00:30:45.409
So if somebody has today has locked in syndrome
and the only way that they can communicate

00:30:45.409 --> 00:30:48.309
is with their eyes you would say is that a
person?

00:30:48.309 --> 00:30:52.029
I think the answer that everybody would say,
the answer to that is yes.

00:30:52.029 --> 00:30:56.340
But if you said that if you transfer that
person's brain into a functioning robot and

00:30:56.340 --> 00:31:01.820
now that person could walk and speak and do
all of the things that they couldn't do when

00:31:01.820 --> 00:31:05.789
they had locked in syndrome is that the same
person?

00:31:05.789 --> 00:31:10.149
It's an ethical question but at some point
that may be the kind of life, if we're really

00:31:10.149 --> 00:31:13.470
talking about immortality, that may be the
question or asking.

00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:17.820
I sense all kinds of potential red flags going
up in the back of my brain.

00:31:17.820 --> 00:31:18.859
As you should.

00:31:18.859 --> 00:31:20.950
And in the front of Joe’s.

00:31:20.950 --> 00:31:23.740
Well you know I think but let's start in the
short term.

00:31:23.740 --> 00:31:28.590
I think you know the history of medicine is
littered, you know, with the more failure

00:31:28.590 --> 00:31:30.519
than success.

00:31:30.519 --> 00:31:34.679
And so you know the classic immortal cell
is the cancer cell.

00:31:34.679 --> 00:31:35.679
Right.

00:31:35.679 --> 00:31:37.109
OK, that doesn't stop dividing.

00:31:37.109 --> 00:31:42.360
So I think that the risk of iatrogenesis and
doing harm, Primum non nocere, above all else

00:31:42.360 --> 00:31:44.649
do no harm to be probably at the forefront.

00:31:44.649 --> 00:31:51.490
I think the skepticism about translating from
animals to humans is indicative of that concern.

00:31:51.490 --> 00:31:58.659
You know longer term I think that that there
are issues of social disruption you know changes

00:31:58.659 --> 00:32:03.779
in longevity, health disparities I think are
a really big issue.

00:32:03.779 --> 00:32:10.649
There's a concern about you know changing
expectations, the science getting ahead of

00:32:10.649 --> 00:32:16.029
social norms, social constructs and this would
be a classic story that we do all the time.

00:32:16.029 --> 00:32:19.519
We have a scientific advance and then we're
trying to catch up with it.

00:32:19.519 --> 00:32:22.419
You know we have a hard time with genetic
testing.

00:32:22.419 --> 00:32:28.599
And I'm worried about sort of the eugenics,
you know, flavor of selecting embryos.

00:32:28.599 --> 00:32:33.159
I think we're you know we're strengthened
by our diversity and there are many people

00:32:33.159 --> 00:32:35.950
in this room who are probably heterozygote
for something bad.

00:32:35.950 --> 00:32:40.599
I would say everybody in this room is heterozygote,
have a good gene and a bad gene you know.

00:32:40.599 --> 00:32:47.720
So if we were selecting for that for the good
genes, a lot of good people will be left out.

00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:51.610
And I think that that really worries me and
that hasn't worked out too well with history.

00:32:51.610 --> 00:32:56.059
So you know and again last point, you know
all these people cite enhancement.

00:32:56.059 --> 00:32:59.989
People who are in favor of enhancement, the
philosophers, some of whom were mostly in

00:32:59.989 --> 00:33:04.419
Oxford you know, are the people who need to
be enhanced the ones who were going to decide

00:33:04.419 --> 00:33:05.419
how we're going to be enhanced?

00:33:05.419 --> 00:33:06.659
Again it’s the humans.

00:33:06.659 --> 00:33:08.179
So I'm very concerned.

00:33:08.179 --> 00:33:13.940
I have no trouble with the medical you know
utility compressing morbidity and improving

00:33:13.940 --> 00:33:16.200
the life span and I think that's all terrific.

00:33:16.200 --> 00:33:21.830
But I start worrying about us engineering
our future as a species in a way that's incredibly

00:33:21.830 --> 00:33:22.830
disruptive.

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:29.059
And this comes in the field of new understanding
of post neo-Darwin thinking about what evolution

00:33:29.059 --> 00:33:30.059
is.

00:33:30.059 --> 00:33:36.450
The old view, which was not Darwin's, was
that it was just simply random mutations and

00:33:36.450 --> 00:33:39.200
struggling for survival of the fittest and
fighting tooth and claw.

00:33:39.200 --> 00:33:44.419
The new view, from what I understand from
what I've been reading lately it's much more

00:33:44.419 --> 00:33:48.869
complex that we are a species that gets ahead
by not just by having winners or losers, but

00:33:48.869 --> 00:33:49.940
by cooperating.

00:33:49.940 --> 00:33:54.330
A lot of cooperation and sharing is what maybe
makes us so much.

00:33:54.330 --> 00:33:58.529
Plus epigenetic factors there are environmental
factors, which are not inheritable.

00:33:58.529 --> 00:33:59.529
Right.

00:33:59.529 --> 00:34:00.529
So Lamarck was right.

00:34:00.529 --> 00:34:02.849
Acquired characteristics should be inherited.

00:34:02.849 --> 00:34:07.890
So this leads to a very difficult, I'm glad
you used the word eugenics because we should

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:13.860
be reminded eugenics was very popular in America
in the tens and twenties and thirties and

00:34:13.860 --> 00:34:19.070
then when they were sterilizing people who
seemed to be imbeciles and a lot of that was

00:34:19.070 --> 00:34:20.130
going on legally.

00:34:20.130 --> 00:34:22.080
Eventually became illegal.

00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:28.240
But people who liked it went over and joined
Hitler’s groups who carried out experiments

00:34:28.240 --> 00:34:29.919
in Auschwitz for example.

00:34:29.919 --> 00:34:36.230
Yeah but I think the science is advancing
and the science, it's not coincidental that

00:34:36.230 --> 00:34:40.440
Jim was talking about that big advances over
the last decade.

00:34:40.440 --> 00:34:46.070
We are at an inflection point in our science
and we are at an inflection point in our evolution

00:34:46.070 --> 00:34:47.159
as a species.

00:34:47.159 --> 00:34:53.929
And so for four billion years our ancestors
have evolved by the forces that you that you've

00:34:53.929 --> 00:34:54.929
mentioned.

00:34:54.929 --> 00:35:01.640
But right now our science is beginning a process
and of allowing us to guide our own evolutionary

00:35:01.640 --> 00:35:02.640
path.

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:05.510
Utopian thinkers have been saying that for
centuries.

00:35:05.510 --> 00:35:06.640
For sure

00:35:06.640 --> 00:35:08.760
It's not a new story.

00:35:08.760 --> 00:35:11.280
Yes, but our tools are so much more advanced

00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:12.280
And they’ve been saying that too.

00:35:12.280 --> 00:35:14.250
And that they're getting more advanced.

00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:16.512
And so but we will still have the same ethical
challenges.

00:35:16.512 --> 00:35:21.680
But the decision-making has not gotten more
advanced is what I was saying.

00:35:21.680 --> 00:35:22.680
We're still human.

00:35:22.680 --> 00:35:28.740
And the people who are in power and the people
who are making those decisions have not necessarily

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:29.920
gotten more advanced either.

00:35:29.920 --> 00:35:31.110
That's one hundred percent correct.

00:35:31.110 --> 00:35:33.930
In fact we're in a post-Enlightenment age…

00:35:33.930 --> 00:35:35.350
We are we are heading in the wrong direction.

00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:37.120
We don't believe in fact, we don’t believe
in science.

00:35:37.120 --> 00:35:38.360
We don’t believe in vaccines.

00:35:38.360 --> 00:35:39.600
There's no doubt about that.

00:35:39.600 --> 00:35:40.830
A plus B equals catastrophe.

00:35:40.830 --> 00:35:41.830
I agree.

00:35:41.830 --> 00:35:43.790
I completely agree with that.

00:35:43.790 --> 00:35:48.700
And yet the science is advancing exponentially
and so the onus, that's why these kinds of

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:52.000
conversations are so important, is the onus
is on all of us.

00:35:52.000 --> 00:35:56.220
Because you can either stop the science and
let's say you stop it here it's going to happen

00:35:56.220 --> 00:36:00.890
someplace else or you can say well we better
really get our act together and if we value

00:36:00.890 --> 00:36:03.070
diversity, let's have a conversation about
diversity

00:36:03.070 --> 00:36:09.080
Back in the 90s I was doing stem cell research
when, when then President Bush said we're

00:36:09.080 --> 00:36:13.700
going to we're going to eliminate embryonic
stem cell research.

00:36:13.700 --> 00:36:18.820
And I remember getting called by the media
and asked how I felt about that and I basically

00:36:18.820 --> 00:36:24.940
said the problem is, if we're only going to
use the established cell lines that were all

00:36:24.940 --> 00:36:30.360
established from rich white people who can
afford in vitro fertilization, guess what?

00:36:30.360 --> 00:36:34.060
We're only really about twelve of those that
are any good.

00:36:34.060 --> 00:36:39.760
So we're going to make all our decisions about
stem cells going forward based on twelve rich

00:36:39.760 --> 00:36:40.980
white people?

00:36:40.980 --> 00:36:46.270
That scares me because diversity is what we
need to understand biology.

00:36:46.270 --> 00:36:53.680
If you look at every one of just us, who aren't
that diverse, we're going to be different.

00:36:53.680 --> 00:36:57.100
Another axis of diversity is people who have
disabilities.

00:36:57.100 --> 00:36:58.940
I couldn't agree more about diversity.

00:36:58.940 --> 00:37:03.300
Diversity is our sole survival strategy as
a species and has been from this from the

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:04.300
beginning.

00:37:04.300 --> 00:37:11.060
So if we lose whatever people think is good,
it's good now it won't be in in the future.

00:37:11.060 --> 00:37:16.790
And with these ethical questions that's at
the essence of the debate that everybody needs

00:37:16.790 --> 00:37:17.790
to be part of.

00:37:17.790 --> 00:37:23.510
This is not a debate for experts it's not
even a debate for just one for one day or

00:37:23.510 --> 00:37:24.510
one weekend.

00:37:24.510 --> 00:37:29.440
I mean these are the questions are the fundamental
questions about the future of our species.

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:33.200
And that that should be the top of everybody's
minds.

00:37:33.200 --> 00:37:38.690
But we can't close our eyes to where this
technology is heading because again we could

00:37:38.690 --> 00:37:40.730
shut this down in the United States.

00:37:40.730 --> 00:37:45.090
It will be good or bad whatever your views
are for the United States but it won't have

00:37:45.090 --> 00:37:47.890
any impact whatsoever on the advance of this
technology.

00:37:47.890 --> 00:37:51.230
The fact that reproductive cloning happens.

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:56.910
The fact that CRISPR technologies that you've
all heard about where you can you can actually,

00:37:56.910 --> 00:38:06.300
you can actually edit someone's genes in real
life says, and it is happening in humans outside

00:38:06.300 --> 00:38:14.350
the US now, is something we all ought to be
discussing and what we, whether we embrace

00:38:14.350 --> 00:38:15.480
that or don't.

00:38:15.480 --> 00:38:19.270
And if you had a time machine and you went
a hundred years into the future and people

00:38:19.270 --> 00:38:21.700
were saying, oh we have this thing, it's made
of code.

00:38:21.700 --> 00:38:25.350
The code is writable, it's readable, attackable
you'd say oh yeah I got you.

00:38:25.350 --> 00:38:27.730
It's computer code and they'd like look at
you like you're crazy.

00:38:27.730 --> 00:38:29.970
No it's biology right?

00:38:29.970 --> 00:38:35.570
We are we are developing the tools to write
and rewrite the code of life.

00:38:35.570 --> 00:38:41.890
We may be wise or not wise in those applications
but we will have those tools.

00:38:41.890 --> 00:38:45.680
And forgive me for asking this question, but
we're talking about people who are lucky enough

00:38:45.680 --> 00:38:46.680
to know about all this stuff.

00:38:46.680 --> 00:38:51.740
Do you think it will be something that can
be spread to all humans everywhere without

00:38:51.740 --> 00:38:54.280
a lot of the expense eventually?

00:38:54.280 --> 00:38:55.280
Eventually.

00:38:55.280 --> 00:38:59.120
Or will leave a lot of humans just dying and
in terrible health?

00:38:59.120 --> 00:39:02.190
I think that again it depends on what our
values are.

00:39:02.190 --> 00:39:05.530
Right now we have all of the food to feed
everybody on Earth.

00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:07.540
We have the clothing to clothe everybody on
Earth.

00:39:07.540 --> 00:39:09.160
We choose not to do so.

00:39:09.160 --> 00:39:15.360
If we maintain the bad values that we collectively
have now that will be the case.

00:39:15.360 --> 00:39:20.210
But there is, there are financial incentives
because eliminating, as Jim was saying, if

00:39:20.210 --> 00:39:26.260
we can eliminate or reduce the occurrence
of these co-morbidities associated with aging

00:39:26.260 --> 00:39:27.960
that's going to be a huge amount of savings.

00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:34.270
If people are living healthy hundred year
lives and then and then die relatively quickly,

00:39:34.270 --> 00:39:38.940
that's going to free a lot of resources that
can be invested in all sorts of things.

00:39:38.940 --> 00:39:42.370
There's a phenomenon, there are people who
are what we call super centenarians.

00:39:42.370 --> 00:39:47.730
These are people who live to a very advanced
age that tends to be inherited in families.

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:52.540
And one of the interesting things about these
people who are dying at one hundred and five,

00:39:52.540 --> 00:39:57.350
one hundred and ten, is that when you look
at large numbers of them they live very, very

00:39:57.350 --> 00:40:01.460
healthy and independent and they tend to be
dead within six months of their first visit

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:04.650
to a doctor, so they die very quickly.

00:40:04.650 --> 00:40:08.860
We also notice this in experimental animals
that are treated with some of these kinds

00:40:08.860 --> 00:40:18.620
of interventions where a single gene mutations
have been used that have an effect on lifespan.

00:40:18.620 --> 00:40:28.510
There are also some data from about twenty
years ago that came from studies of health

00:40:28.510 --> 00:40:29.510
utilization.

00:40:29.510 --> 00:40:34.290
That the last two years of life are the most
expensive.

00:40:34.290 --> 00:40:39.470
They're three, the last two years of life
of a person who dies at seventy cost three

00:40:39.470 --> 00:40:44.220
times the last two years of life of someone
who dies on a hundred.

00:40:44.220 --> 00:40:50.560
And that may in part because of this rectangularization
of morbidity.

00:40:50.560 --> 00:40:52.570
So I've got a shorter-term view of some of
this.

00:40:52.570 --> 00:40:55.520
I've got patients here now.

00:40:55.520 --> 00:41:01.520
My view is I want to extend their healthspan
and delay the onset of these diseases.

00:41:01.520 --> 00:41:06.620
And it's turning out now that these interventions
will actually treat some of these diseases

00:41:06.620 --> 00:41:09.420
once there's established including fragility,
in animals.

00:41:09.420 --> 00:41:10.700
Sorry to interrupt.

00:41:10.700 --> 00:41:11.700
Healthspan.

00:41:11.700 --> 00:41:13.270
You've used that term twice.

00:41:13.270 --> 00:41:16.800
A lovely phrase, healthspan as opposed to
lifespan.

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:21.260
Yes, so healthspan is a period during life
when people are living independently, free

00:41:21.260 --> 00:41:24.300
of pain, free of disability.

00:41:24.300 --> 00:41:27.150
You know basically able to do what they what
they want to do.

00:41:27.150 --> 00:41:33.610
So the goal of the field, as far as I can
read it from my vantage point, is that we

00:41:33.610 --> 00:41:38.119
want to improve healthspan and if lifestyle
is increased that's a side effect.

00:41:38.119 --> 00:41:42.480
So a question about the fact that these things
seem to run in families, parents, grandparents

00:41:42.480 --> 00:41:44.320
going back that way.

00:41:44.320 --> 00:41:49.140
But is it necessarily something that runs
in families because it's passed down genetically,

00:41:49.140 --> 00:41:55.090
or genetically and epigenetically, or both
of those plus culturally, and just something

00:41:55.090 --> 00:41:56.720
about the lifestyle?

00:41:56.720 --> 00:42:05.550
This has been noted across populations in
different countries and I'm talking about

00:42:05.550 --> 00:42:08.470
people who live beyond, way beyond a hundred.

00:42:08.470 --> 00:42:14.580
So there are several trials looking at these
kinds of populations going on around the world.

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:19.230
There's a high heritable component but it's
not everything, so of course they're going

00:42:19.230 --> 00:42:22.900
to be epigenetic and social and other kinds
of factors involved.

00:42:22.900 --> 00:42:30.630
But some of the genetic changes that account
for or contribute to this increased longevity

00:42:30.630 --> 00:42:33.150
within families are beginning to be worked
out.

00:42:33.150 --> 00:42:38.940
There are about a dozen, a dozen polymorphisms
we call them that have been found so far and

00:42:38.940 --> 00:42:43.800
they make sense because they follow the same
pathways of food restriction which extends

00:42:43.800 --> 00:42:52.220
lifespan in many species and they contain
the targets of these kinds of drugs that are

00:42:52.220 --> 00:42:54.780
being developed that affect lifespan.

00:42:54.780 --> 00:42:55.780
So that sounds hopeful.

00:42:55.780 --> 00:43:00.160
Eventually you may be able to get to understand
this well enough to be able to spread the

00:43:00.160 --> 00:43:01.470
knowledge to everybody.

00:43:01.470 --> 00:43:02.920
That's the idea.

00:43:02.920 --> 00:43:08.220
And as we were talking about before, some
of the early drugs that may have an effect...and

00:43:08.220 --> 00:43:09.220
don't take these drugs.

00:43:09.220 --> 00:43:10.220
Don't take them.

00:43:10.220 --> 00:43:11.220
We haven't done the trials yet.

00:43:11.220 --> 00:43:12.590
Don't take these drugs.

00:43:12.590 --> 00:43:14.790
They’re all taking their paper out.

00:43:14.790 --> 00:43:17.670
It happens every time somebody says where
do I get this.

00:43:17.670 --> 00:43:18.980
Don't don't.

00:43:18.980 --> 00:43:24.090
Some of them are very, very cheap like Metformin
we were talking before, two to five cents

00:43:24.090 --> 00:43:26.400
a pill.

00:43:26.400 --> 00:43:31.720
So if we are able and in the next iteration
after we do these short term kinds of studies

00:43:31.720 --> 00:43:38.710
and look at individual indications and see
if they in aggregate line up, with the possibility

00:43:38.710 --> 00:43:44.010
we're targeting fundamental aging processes,
we may move to kind of preventive strategy.

00:43:44.010 --> 00:43:52.940
And if that happened to work, if that happened
to work, it would by projections that have

00:43:52.940 --> 00:43:59.570
been done by a variety of groups, reduce health
care costs enough that the intervention itself

00:43:59.570 --> 00:44:04.270
would be relatively much cheaper for any society.

00:44:04.270 --> 00:44:07.820
Could I just, Maybe you guys could just talk
a little bit about the role of inflammation

00:44:07.820 --> 00:44:12.030
in aging because I think that that would be
really interesting to kind of get on the table.

00:44:12.030 --> 00:44:17.730
Well chronic, what we call sterile, that is
in the absence of bacteria or viruses, low

00:44:17.730 --> 00:44:22.840
grade inflammation is something that you find
in many tissues and sites of age related disease.

00:44:22.840 --> 00:44:27.890
So this is where immune cells go and release
factors that cause dysfunction.

00:44:27.890 --> 00:44:31.880
Senescent cells act in part by producing a
lot of inflammatory factors and also attract

00:44:31.880 --> 00:44:34.490
cells from the immune system in.

00:44:34.490 --> 00:44:39.990
And these kinds of factors will also poison
stem and progenitor cells and cause them to

00:44:39.990 --> 00:44:44.600
dysfunction and can contribute to macromolecular
dysfunction comes with DNA and proteins so

00:44:44.600 --> 00:44:47.080
all these things are sort of interlinked.

00:44:47.080 --> 00:44:49.140
But this kind of inflammation is very special
inflammation.

00:44:49.140 --> 00:44:53.350
It's not the kind of inflammation you would
treat with a non-steroidal anti-inflammatory

00:44:53.350 --> 00:44:55.190
drug or with aspirin.

00:44:55.190 --> 00:45:02.890
Although aspirin does in mice and related
drugs, salicylate, does have some effect on

00:45:02.890 --> 00:45:07.480
median lifespan at least in lower mammals.

00:45:07.480 --> 00:45:13.400
What we're talking about is inflammation that's
much more specific and of a particular type

00:45:13.400 --> 00:45:17.150
and Metformin is one of the drugs that targets
that kind of inflammation, rapamycin is another

00:45:17.150 --> 00:45:18.650
one that does it.

00:45:18.650 --> 00:45:22.700
So these are these are inflammatory mediators
produced by senescent cells, something called

00:45:22.700 --> 00:45:25.120
senescence associated secretory phenotype.

00:45:25.120 --> 00:45:26.640
Long word, we call it SASP.

00:45:26.640 --> 00:45:30.250
That form in part works by targeting that
pathway.

00:45:30.250 --> 00:45:31.250
Doris?

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:36.450
So I want to speak to that though because
I, my worldview is slightly different which

00:45:36.450 --> 00:45:40.860
is that acute inflammation is a good thing.

00:45:40.860 --> 00:45:48.960
Acute inflammation is when an injury occurs
and basically your body is producing a signal

00:45:48.960 --> 00:45:54.870
saying ‘hey there's an injury’, and I
need repair, whatever that repair happens

00:45:54.870 --> 00:45:56.220
to be.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:46:00.730
And I happen to believe part of that is these
cells that you recruit, they're not all stem

00:46:00.730 --> 00:46:05.530
and progenitor cells, some of them are immune
cells, so you get those cells there.

00:46:05.530 --> 00:46:11.790
A number of years ago we started look....and
if you get those cells there you turn off

00:46:11.790 --> 00:46:13.500
the inflammation.

00:46:13.500 --> 00:46:19.680
If you don't, your body ramps up inflammation
and I think it's like your body saying ‘I

00:46:19.680 --> 00:46:22.770
said I've got an injury send me cells!’

00:46:22.770 --> 00:46:28.980
And you start recruiting the wrong cells and
getting a secondary pro-inflammatory effect.

00:46:28.980 --> 00:46:36.900
A number of years ago we started when people
started doing stem cell therapy trials, that

00:46:36.900 --> 00:46:43.280
were primarily bone marrow derived stem cells,
many of you may have heard you get bone marrow

00:46:43.280 --> 00:46:47.460
from your hip, you take the cells and you
give 'em and you're giving stem cells.

00:46:47.460 --> 00:46:53.510
Well only three percent of the cells in our
bone marrow are stem cells, the other ninety

00:46:53.510 --> 00:47:00.890
seven percent are not stem cells they're immune
cells or other types of cells that are going

00:47:00.890 --> 00:47:06.450
to give rise to blood cells or etcetera.

00:47:06.450 --> 00:47:12.880
And when we started measuring those and said
which cells that are present associate with

00:47:12.880 --> 00:47:14.520
